<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/1st-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Northern Crusades</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">12th–15th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Baltic Crusades · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">Crusades</a> were the crusades waged by Catholic Danish, Swedish and German rulers and military orders against the pagan peoples around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a> from the 12th to the 15th century. They included the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wendish-crusade">Wendish Crusade</a> of 1147, the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a>'s subjugation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, and were later directed against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> Novgorod. They brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and German settlement to the eastern Baltic.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Northern Crusades were military campaigns by Catholic kingdoms and military orders to convert the Balts, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finns">Finns</a> and West Slavs around the southern and eastern shores of the Baltic. Some were explicitly called crusades, such as the war on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonians">Estonians</a> authorised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-alexander-iii">Pope Alexander III</a>'s bull of 1171, and some, like the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wendish-crusade">Wendish Crusade</a>, partly aimed at control of rich resources. Pope Celestine III's call in 1195 is counted as the official start, though the kingdoms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> had begun earlier. Conflict between Finnic, Baltic and Slavic peoples and their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> and Danish neighbours had been common for centuries, and the crusades gave it a papal sanction.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wendish-crusade">Wendish Crusade</a> of 1147 ran alongside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-crusade">Second Crusade</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polabian-slavs">Polabian Slavs</a>. Sweden campaigned against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finns">Finns</a>, Tavastians and Karelians between 1150 and 1293, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusades</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>, including one by Canute VI in 1191.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a>, Bishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-of-riga">Albert of Buxhoeveden</a> landed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-dvina">Daugava</a> in 1200, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonian-brothers-of-the-sword">Livonian Brothers of the Sword</a> were founded in 1202. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonians">Livonians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latgalians">Latgallians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonians">Estonians</a> were subdued in turn, and Estonian resistance ended after a defeat near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viljandi">Viljandi</a> in 1217 in which the leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lembitu">Lembitu</a> was killed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> landed at Lindanisse, later Tallinn, in 1219, and Saaremaa accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> in 1227 after an army of 20,000 crossed the frozen sea. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semigallians">Semigallians</a> were conquered only in 1289 and 1290.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konrad-i-of-masovia">Konrad I of Masovia</a> called in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a> in 1226 after his own attempts failed. The Brothers of the Sword were crushed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samogitians">Samogitians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semigallians">Semigallians</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-saule">Battle of Saule</a> in 1236 and were absorbed into the Teutonic Order in 1237 as the Livonian Order. The Order failed to conquer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, though King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindaugas">Mindaugas</a> accepted baptism around 1251. Against Orthodox Novgorod the Livonian branch was defeated at the Battle of the Ice in 1242.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> began its official conversion with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-ii-jagie-o">Jogaila</a>'s baptism in 1386 and his marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jadwiga-of-poland">Jadwiga of Poland</a>. Fighting continued until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-grunwald">Battle of Grunwald</a> in 1410, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and Lithuania decisively defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a>. The pagan peoples suffered forced baptism and military occupation, while the Teutonic Order and German merchants profited greatly. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a>, also called Terra Mariana, remained under the complex control of the Livonian Order and prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Northern Crusades” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their pagan culture was “still in full working order” in the 11th century, when an uprising broke out in the 1030s, but in the 12th century Catholicism was imposed by force in the <b>Northern Crusades</b>, bound up with Germanisation.</blockquote><a href="/religion/slavic-paganism">Slavic paganism · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Charlemagne forced the Saxons to convert, the Norwegian kings Olaf Tryggvason and Olaf Haraldsson imposed Christianity, often by force, and in the 12th century the <b>Northern Crusades</b> imposed Catholicism on the Baltic West Slavs.</blockquote><a href="/essays/faiths-on-the-move">Faiths on the Move</a></li></ul></section>
